<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ichijōdani</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ichijodani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ichijōdani is a narrow valley near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, where the Asakura clan built their castle and town in the late 15th century. Under five generations of Asakura rulers it became a prosperous cultural centre that welcomed refugee courtiers and scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> burned it in 1573, and the excavated ruins, with restored streets and gardens, are a protected historic site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ichijodani.jpg" alt="Ichijōdani" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asakura_Yakata_of_Ichijodani_Asakura_Family_Historic_Ruins02s3s4440.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ichijōdani is the valley of a branch of the Asuwa River, about 500 metres wide from east to west and about three kilometres long, in the Kidonouchi section of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a>. Mountains enclose it on the east, west and south and the river on the north, so that it formed a natural fortification.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1471 the Asakura displaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiba-clan">Shiba clan</a> as shugo, or military commanders, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echizen-province">Echizen Province</a>, and that year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asakura-toshikage">Asakura Toshikage</a> (1428–1481) fortified the valley with hilltop forts on the mountains around it and walls and gates at its northern and southern ends. Within them stood his fortified mansion, the homes of his relatives and retainers, and later the houses of merchants and artisans and Buddhist temples. People of culture and skill fleeing the Ōnin War found refuge there, and by the time of Asakura Takakage (1493–1548) the town had over 10,000 inhabitants.</p><p>The Asakura kept good relations with the Ashikaga shogunate, which brought them into conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>. After Nobunaga took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, Shōgun Ashikaga Yoshiaki asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asakura-yoshikage">Asakura Yoshikage</a>, head of the clan since 1548, to help drive him out. Nobunaga invaded Echizen, won at the Siege of Kanegasaki and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-anegawa">Battle of Anegawa</a> in 1570, and razed Ichijōdani in the Siege of Ichijōdani Castle in 1573, ending 103 years of Asakura rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Excavation began in 1967 and has uncovered the whole town: the lord's house, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> residences, temples, the houses of merchants and craft workers, and the streets. Samurai and merchant houses have been restored along a street 200 metres long, and four gardens have been partly restored. Of about 1,700,000 relics found, 2,343 are Important Cultural Properties, many shown at the Ichijodani Asakura Family Site Museum. The whole site of 278 hectares was designated a Special Historic Site in 1971.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ichijōdani Asakura Family Historic Ruins” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He built his seat in the narrow valley of <b>Ichijodani</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/asakura-clan-domain">Asakura clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
